WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.380
Mechanical compatibility depends on reference geometry, not just overall width.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.360
A datum is the reference used to locate a feature.

3
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.380
When two drawings use different references, apparently similar dimensions can describe different

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:12.920
positions.

5
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.160
Read the datum, orientation and tolerance before comparing numbers.

6
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.760
The O R V three base specification allows alternative frame geometries and

7
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.880
leaves external dimensions to the rack developer.

8
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.400
The shared interfaces still have to agree.

9
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.080
Identify the selected option and use its drawing consistently.

10
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.440
Don't combine one option's support geometry with another option's connection location.

11
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.060
A keep-out is reserved space, not unused space available for an extra

12
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:39.480
bracket.

13
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.120
It can protect connector travel, tubing movement, tools or a replacement path.

14
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.200
Check the equipment in its installed and service positions, including accumulated tolerances

15
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.280
and allowable deflection.

16
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.800
A tray can rest on its supports while its rear interfaces are

17
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.240
outside their permitted alignment.

18
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.680
Treat the source drawing as the controlling geometry; the simplified course figure

19
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.980
is a reading aid, not a fabrication drawing.

